<body>
    <h1>Training an Action Classifier</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <div class=3D"contentLayout2">
<div class=3D"columnLayout fixed-width" data-layout=3D"fixed-width">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<p>An&nbsp;<strong>Action Classifier</strong>&nbsp;is a machine learning mo=
del designed to identify and classify human actions from video footage. Thi=
s type of classifier leverages computational vision technologies, primarily=
 Vision and Create ML, to recognize body movements and patterns. For instan=
ce, an action classifier trained to detect exercise movements can recognize=
 and label videos of someone performing jumping jacks, squats, or lunges.</=
p>
<p>In this section, you will learn how to create, train, and deploy an acti=
on classifier using Swift and Create ML. We=E2=80=99ll cover every essentia=
l step=E2=80=94from gathering and preparing data to evaluating and integrat=
ing the classifier into an iOS application.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-WhyUseanActionClassifier?">Why Use an =
Action Classifier?</h3>
<p>Implementing action classifiers can significantly enhance your app by en=
abling it to automatically recognize physical activities, movements, or ges=
tures. Common application areas include:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Fitness Apps:</strong>&nbsp;Automatic recognition of workout rou=
tines and tracking user progress.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Interactive Games:</strong>&nbsp;Detecting player gestures to fa=
cilitate immersive gameplay.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Augmented Reality (AR):</strong>&nbsp;Enabling realistic interac=
tion between users and virtual objects.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-ActionClassifier">Action Classifier</h=
3>
<p>Build a customized action recognition model using Create ML by collectin=
g sample videos showcasing the specific actions you'd like your classifier =
to recognize. For instance, to develop a fitness action recognition model, =
record or collect videos of people performing different workouts like jumpi=
ng jacks, push-ups, squats, or lunges.</p>
<p>During training, Create ML utilizes Apple's&nbsp;Vision framework&nbsp;t=
o pinpoint essential anatomical landmarks on the human body within each vid=
eo frame. Action recognition models are trained to detect and interpret the=
 movement trajectories of these points throughout video sequences.</p>
<p>The Create ML developer tool streamlines the training, evaluation, and t=
esting of your action recognition models. It enables the creation of severa=
l models within one project by defining individual&nbsp;<em>model sources</=
em>, each consisting of specific training data and configuration parameters=
. After achieving your desired performance, export the trained action recog=
nition model as a Core ML file, making it ready for integration into your X=
code project.</p>
<p>At runtime, your app leverages the action recognition model to classify =
actions by analyzing sequential video frames sourced from a live camera fee=
d or pre-recorded video files.</p>
<p>Although training an action recognition model follows a workflow similar=
 to other Create ML models, such as image classifiers, there are distinct c=
onsiderations to keep in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Setting the appropriate frame rate for the action recognition model base=
d on the intended use in your app.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Ensuring video examples match or surpass this frame rate for optimal acc=
uracy.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Capturing videos of individuals clearly performing designated actions wi=
thin appropriate environmental conditions.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Including videos depicting closely related yet irrelevant actions to hel=
p the model distinguish and improve accuracy.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-SelectinganAppropriateFrameRate"><stro=
ng>Selecting an Appropriate Frame Rate</strong></h3>
<p>Before creating your action recognition model, determine the frame rate =
(number of frames per second) your destination app will use, whether source=
d from a camera or video file.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>The chosen frame rate significantly impacts your action recognition mode=
l=E2=80=99s effectiveness and the type of training data required.</p>
<p>Aim to align your action recognition model=E2=80=99s frame rate with tha=
t of your target application. For example, if your app captures video at 30=
 frames per second (fps), your action model should also be configured for 3=
0 fps.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-GatheringExampleVideos"><strong>Gather=
ing Example Videos</strong></h3>
<p>After establishing the appropriate frame rate for your action model, col=
lect relevant training videos. While your model=E2=80=99s and application=
=E2=80=99s frame rates must align, the frame rates of the training videos c=
an be equal to or higher. For instance, videos captured at 30, 50, or 60 fp=
s can effectively train a model configured for 30 fps.</p>
<p>Collect a minimum of 50 videos for each action you want your model to re=
cognize. Each training video should clearly feature a single person perform=
ing the action. If capturing videos with multiple individuals, ensure the t=
arget individual performing the action occupies the majority of the frame.<=
/p>
<p>Additionally, gather videos representing a&nbsp;<em>negative class</em>=
=E2=80=94actions that may be similar but irrelevant to your application's c=
ontext. Including negative classes helps your model avoid misclassification=
 by clearly distinguishing between relevant and irrelevant actions.</p>
<p>Proper data organization helps streamline training. Create ML supports s=
ome common methods, such as:</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"columnLayout two-equal" data-layout=3D"two-equal">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p><strong>Labeled Folders:</strong></p>
<p>Store videos in separate labeled folders within a parent directory. Each=
 folder corresponds to a specific action (e.g., Squats, Lunges, Jumping Jac=
ks).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p><strong>Annotation-based:</strong></p>
<p>Store videos in a single folder accompanied by an annotation file (CSV) =
specifying each video's associated action labels.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"columnLayout fixed-width" data-layout=3D"fixed-width">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<p><strong>Configure an Action Classification Project</strong></p>
<p>Open the developer tool by choosing Xcode &gt; Open Developer Tool &gt; =
Create ML, and create a new Action Classification project.</p><span class=
=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedd=
ed-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external=
-resource image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.=
apple.com/published/38c7f28b647dbfccbc2990468c340a44/creating-an-action-cla=
ssifier-model-4@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.app=
le.com/published/38c7f28b647dbfccbc2990468c340a44/creating-an-action-classi=
fier-model-4@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>In the Data section of the model source=E2=80=99s Settings tab, drag the=
 parent folder of your training data source onto the Training Data box.</p>=
<span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper conflu=
ence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluen=
ce-external-resource image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"https://docs-assets=
.developer.apple.com/published/b601f871a91b39358acd52baf7046aad/creating-an=
-action-classifier-model-5@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"https://docs-assets.de=
veloper.apple.com/published/b601f871a91b39358acd52baf7046aad/creating-an-ac=
tion-classifier-model-5@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>If relevant, drag and drop the folders containing your validation and te=
sting datasets into their corresponding Validation Data and Testing Data fi=
elds. In case a validation dataset isn't specified, Create ML automatically=
 reserves a portion of the training dataset for validation purposes.</p>
<p>Next, customize your action classifier by adjusting settings in the Para=
meters area. Ensure the Frame Rate matches the frame rate used by your targ=
et application. For instance, if your app captures and processes video at 3=
0 frames per second, you should also set the classifier's Frame Rate parame=
ter to 30 fps.</p>
<p>Finally, set the Action Duration according to how long it typically take=
s to perform actions in your training videos. If most actions in your datas=
et are completed in roughly two seconds, select an Action Duration of 2 sec=
onds.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapp=
er confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image=
 confluence-external-resource image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"https://do=
cs-assets.developer.apple.com/published/6c35996d1b5e528e7c54e38cfd989e17/cr=
eating-an-action-classifier-model-6@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"https://docs-=
assets.developer.apple.com/published/6c35996d1b5e528e7c54e38cfd989e17/creat=
ing-an-action-classifier-model-6@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>Create ML determines the prediction window size=E2=80=94the total number=
 of frames required for each prediction=E2=80=94by multiplying the chosen F=
rame Rate by the Action Duration. For example, a Frame Rate of 30 fps and a=
n Action Duration of 2 seconds would result in a prediction window size of =
60 frames.</p>
<p>If the actions you're classifying are equally recognizable from either t=
he left or right side of the camera view, you can significantly enhance you=
r training dataset by activating the Horizontal Flip augmentation. When Hor=
izontal Flip is turned on, Create ML generates horizontally mirrored versio=
ns of the landmark positions identified by Vision for each analyzed video f=
rame.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-TrainingYourActionClassifier"><strong>=
Training Your Action Classifier</strong></h3>
<p>Click the&nbsp;<strong>Train</strong>&nbsp;button to initiate the traini=
ng process. Create ML begins by extracting features, employing a&nbsp;<code=
>VNDetectHumanBodyPoseRequest</code>&nbsp;to identify body landmarks within=
 every frame of your video data.</p>
<p>This initial feature extraction phase might require some time, depending=
 on both your dataset's size and your Mac's computing capabilities. Once fe=
ature extraction is complete, Create ML moves into the training phase. Duri=
ng this stage, it trains the action classifier to recognize specific action=
s from patterns of landmark data. Throughout the training process, Create M=
L provides visual feedback, displaying a graph of the model=E2=80=99s accur=
acy improvements across successive training iterations.</p><span class=3D"c=
onfluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-ma=
nual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external-reso=
urce image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple=
.com/published/eec07bf4c375b3814a66d3539f0cae29/creating-an-action-classifi=
er-model-7@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple.co=
m/published/eec07bf4c375b3814a66d3539f0cae29/creating-an-action-classifier-=
model-7@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>If you need to pause the training session temporarily=E2=80=94for instan=
ce, to conserve battery life=E2=80=94click the&nbsp;<strong>Pause</strong>&=
nbsp;button. When you're prepared to resume, simply click&nbsp;<strong>Resu=
me</strong>.</p>
<p>To test an early version of your model without waiting for the entire tr=
aining session to complete, select&nbsp;<strong>Snapshot</strong>. Snapshot=
s let you export preliminary Core ML model files. To export a snapshot, sel=
ect it and use the options available in the&nbsp;<strong>Output</strong>&nb=
sp;tab.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-EvaluatingYourActionClassifier"><stron=
g>Evaluating Your Action Classifier</strong></h3>
<p>Review your trained model=E2=80=99s predictive performance by examining =
the&nbsp;<strong>Recall</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>Precision</strong>&n=
bsp;metrics displayed within the&nbsp;<strong>Evaluation</strong>&nbsp;tab.=
 These metrics are available separately for the training, validation, and t=
esting stages.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-cen=
ter-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embed=
ded-image confluence-external-resource image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"h=
ttps://docs-assets.developer.apple.com/published/b0e16a86bb370138f9c009b1b4=
8eff32/creating-an-action-classifier-model-8@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"http=
s://docs-assets.developer.apple.com/published/b0e16a86bb370138f9c009b1b48ef=
f32/creating-an-action-classifier-model-8@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>If the action classifier doesn=E2=80=99t meet your needs, click Train Mo=
re to further train the model. If the additional training iterations don=E2=
=80=99t improve the action classifier=E2=80=99s performance.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>you can select File &gt; New Model Source to train a model with either o=
r both of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>A new or modified training-data source</p></li>
<li>
<p>Different parameters</p></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<p>If you=E2=80=99d like to improve the accuracy of your action classifier,=
 consider adjusting the&nbsp;<strong>Action Duration</strong>&nbsp;setting =
or enabling the&nbsp;<strong>Horizontal Flip</strong>&nbsp;augmentation. If=
 the model struggles with recognizing certain actions, enhance your dataset=
 by adding or updating the data source with more varied, high-quality examp=
les specifically demonstrating those challenging actions.</p>
<p>In situations where the model incorrectly labels unrelated movements or =
nonactions as valid actions, it's helpful to create or expand a&nbsp;<stron=
g>negative class</strong>=E2=80=94a collection of videos illustrating examp=
les the classifier should ignore.</p>
<p>After making adjustments to your dataset or parameters, click&nbsp;<stro=
ng>Train</strong>&nbsp;again to build a revised action classifier. Continue=
 iteratively evaluating, fine-tuning, and retraining your classifier until =
you achieve results that meet your expectations.</p>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-PreviewingYourActionClassifier"><stron=
g>Previewing Your Action Classifier</strong></h3>
<p>The&nbsp;<strong>Preview</strong>&nbsp;tab allows you to quickly test an=
d visualize the classifier=E2=80=99s predictions before integrating it into=
 your Xcode project. Simply drag a video file into the Preview area and pre=
ss&nbsp;<strong>Play</strong>&nbsp;to observe how effectively the model ide=
ntifies actions in real-time.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wr=
apper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"c=
onfluence-embedded-image confluence-external-resource image-center" width=
=3D"650" src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple.com/published/78af64f15=
8184d826427091ea163ffc7/creating-an-action-classifier-model-9@2x.png" data-=
image-src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple.com/published/78af64f15818=
4d826427091ea163ffc7/creating-an-action-classifier-model-9@2x.png" loading=
=3D"lazy"></span>
<p>When you drop a video file into the Preview tab, Create ML analyzes the =
entire video using your action classifier. As you play back the video, Crea=
te ML visually displays real-time predictions for each frame, helping you a=
ssess how accurately actions are detected.</p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>To rapidly verify your classifier's capability to recognize all action c=
ategories, use an action montage video that demonstrates each action sequen=
tially.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-ExportingYourActionClassifier"><strong=
>Exporting Your Action Classifier</strong></h3>
<p>To export your trained action classifier as a Core ML model file, naviga=
te to the&nbsp;<strong>Output</strong>&nbsp;tab and choose from the availab=
le options:&nbsp;<strong>Get</strong>,&nbsp;<strong>Xcode</strong>, or&nbsp=
;<strong>Share</strong>. You can export a fully-trained model or a snapshot=
 captured at any stage during the training process.</p><span class=3D"confl=
uence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual=
-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image confluence-external-resource=
 image-center" width=3D"650" src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple.com=
/published/72e5488326ec24c5ab791b2dfa302abb/creating-an-action-classifier-m=
odel-10@2x.png" data-image-src=3D"https://docs-assets.developer.apple.com/p=
ublished/72e5488326ec24c5ab791b2dfa302abb/creating-an-action-classifier-mod=
el-10@2x.png" loading=3D"lazy"></span>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-IntegratingtheClassifierintoYourApp">I=
ntegrating the Classifier into Your App</h3>
<p>Once satisfied with the classifier=E2=80=99s performance, export it as a=
 Core ML&nbsp;<code>.mlmodel</code>&nbsp;file and add it to your Xcode proj=
ect:</p>
<p>Here's a Swift code example of integrating the model using the Vision fr=
amework:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import V=
ision
import CoreML

let model =3D try VNCoreMLModel(for: ActionClassifier().model)

let request =3D VNCoreMLRequest(model: model) { request, error in
    guard let results =3D request.results as? [VNClassificationObservation]=
,
          let topResult =3D results.first else { return }

    print("Action detected: \\(topResult.identifier) with confidence \\(top=
Result.confidence)")
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>As this article has focus on training, lets not go deep into how to depl=
oy the trained model.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"TraininganActionClassifier-Conclusion">Conclusion</h3>
<p>Training an action classifier in Swift involves a detailed workflow from=
 data preparation to model training and integration. By leveraging Create M=
L for training and Core ML along with Vision for deployment, iOS developers=
 can build powerful applications capable of real-time action recognition. T=
his section provided a comprehensive guide, highlighting best practices and=
 code examples that serve as a solid foundation for further exploration and=
 refinement in computational vision projects.</p>
<hr>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
------=_Part_32_1188616392.1780722668908--
